// Practice quiz: Debugging with DevTools breakpoints
// First draft: unreviewed. Formative, low-stakes retention practice. Titles name the objective id each item practises.

$CATEGORY: html-css-js/dom-tooling-and-async/debugging-with-devtools-breakpoints

::o1 conditional use::When is a conditional breakpoint a better choice than a plain one? {
~When the code has no loops or repeats #Not quite. A plain breakpoint already pauses there exactly once.
~When DevTools is closed #Not quite. Breakpoints need DevTools open.
=When only one pass of a repeated line matters #Correct. It skips every pass where the condition is false.
~When you want to change the source permanently #Not quite. Breakpoints change no source.
}

::o1 debugger statement::What does the debugger statement do when DevTools is closed in normal browsing? {
~It throws an error #Not quite. It does not throw.
=Nothing visible, and the code keeps running #Correct. It only pauses when a debugger is attached.
~It opens DevTools automatically #Not quite. It never opens the tools for you.
~It stops the page permanently #Not quite. It has no such effect.
}

::o2 step over::Paused on a line calling total(), you press Step over. Where do you stop? {
~On the first line inside total #Not quite. That is Step into.
~At the end of the program #Not quite. That is closer to Resume.
~On the previous line #Not quite. Debuggers do not step backward.
=On the next line, after total has fully run #Correct. The call runs without pausing inside it, unless there is another breakpoint.
}

::o2 step out::You are paused inside a helper function and have seen enough. Which control returns you to its caller? {
=Step out #Correct. It finishes the current function, and stops in the caller.
~Step into #Not quite. That goes deeper.
~Step over #Not quite. That moves one line within the helper.
~Blackbox #Not quite. That hides a script from stepping; it does not return.
}

::o3 evidence::In a loop, total is 6 after pass one and 10 after pass two, where you expected 16. What does this point to? {
=The total is being replaced rather than added to #Correct. If it were accumulating, it would have grown by 10.
~The second item has the wrong price #Not quite. Its line total of 10 was correct by itself.
~The loop ran too many times #Not quite. It ran twice, as expected.
~The debugger is changing values #Not quite. Pausing does not change them.
}
